  "source_evidence": [
    {
      "location": "Raw PDF page 30; printed page 8; Section 1.4, first paragraph",
      "anchor": "each grid cell can be assigned different material properties via an appropriate averaging of the material parameters over the volume enclosed by the cell",
      "observation": "The selected claim describes assigning material properties to individual finite-volume grid cells by appropriately averaging material parameters over their enclosed volumes. It does not give an averaging formula."
    },
    {
      "location": "Raw PDF page 29; printed page 7; Section 1.4, final paragraph",
      "anchor": "materials that are not homogeneous and isotropic",
      "observation": "The application context is wave propagation in heterogeneous materials. The paragraph discusses both slowly varying heterogeneity and sharp interfaces where material parameters have jump discontinuities."
    },
    {
      "location": "Raw PDF page 29; printed page 7; Section 1.4, final sentence",
      "anchor": "many high-order methods can have difficulties near interfaces, where the solution is typically not smooth",
      "observation": "Interface-related lack of smoothness motivates the discussion of finite-volume methods in the following paragraph."
    },
    {
      "location": "Raw PDF page 30; printed page 8; Section 1.4, first paragraph",
      "anchor": "high-resolution finite volume methods based on solving Riemann problems can be an attractive alternative",
      "observation": "The cellwise material assignment is presented as a reason finite-volume methods are a natural choice for heterogeneous media, within a qualitative discussion of suitability."
    },
    {
      "location": "Raw PDF page 27; printed page 5; Section 1.2, first paragraph",
      "anchor": "the cell average of q, which is this integral divided by the volume of the cell",
      "observation": "For the solution quantity q, the source explicitly defines a cell average as the integral over the grid cell divided by its volume. This definition concerns q; the selected material-parameter passage instead says appropriate averaging without specifying its formula."
    },
    {
      "location": "Raw PDF page 30; printed page 8; Section 1.4, first paragraph",
      "anchor": "a discontinuity in the medium at x = 0 as well as a discontinuity in the initial data",
      "observation": "The surrounding paragraph extends the Riemann-problem idea to discontinuous media. Solving the interface problem decomposes data into waves entering each cell and includes reflection and transmission effects."
    }
  ],
  "statement": {
    "binders": [
      "Each grid cell in a finite-volume discretization of a heterogeneous medium is the object receiving assigned material properties; this is prose-level scope, without a formal index set or quantified theorem (raw PDF page 30, printed page 8, Section 1.4: 'each grid cell can be assigned').",
      "The material parameters over the volume enclosed by that cell are the quantities to be averaged; their mathematical types and number are unspecified (raw PDF page 30, printed page 8, Section 1.4: 'averaging of the material parameters over the volume enclosed by the cell')."
    ],
    "hypotheses": [
      "The stated application is a heterogeneous medium represented using finite-volume grid cells (raw PDF page 30, printed page 8, Section 1.4: 'a natural choice for heterogeneous media, since each grid cell').",
      "Surrounding context includes slowly varying material heterogeneity and sharp material interfaces; jump discontinuities are an included situation, not an explicit requirement for every cellwise assignment (raw PDF page 29, printed page 7, Section 1.4: 'The heterogeneity may be slowly varying' and 'sharp interfaces between different materials')."
    ],
    "conclusions": [
      "Each grid cell can receive material properties obtained through an appropriate averaging of material parameters over its enclosed volume (raw PDF page 30, printed page 8, Section 1.4: 'each grid cell can be assigned different material properties via an appropriate averaging').",
      "This ability to assign material properties cell by cell is offered as a reason finite-volume methods are a natural choice for heterogeneous media (raw PDF page 30, printed page 8, Section 1.4: 'a natural choice for heterogeneous media, since')."
    ],
    "definitions_and_conventions": [
      "The averaging region is the volume enclosed by the individual grid cell (raw PDF page 30, printed page 8, Section 1.4: 'over the volume enclosed by the cell').",
      "In the earlier definition for the solution quantity q, a cell average is its integral over the grid cell divided by the cell volume (raw PDF page 27, printed page 5, Section 1.2: 'this integral divided by the volume of the cell').",
      "The selected material-parameter passage uses the qualification 'appropriate' and provides no corresponding explicit operator, normalization, or formula (raw PDF page 30, printed page 8, Section 1.4: 'an appropriate averaging of the material parameters')."
    ],
    "implicit_context": [
      "The discussion concerns numerical treatment of wave propagation in heterogeneous media, including acoustic and seismic applications (raw PDF page 29, printed page 7, Section 1.4, final paragraph: 'wave-propagation problems' and 'acoustic or seismic waves').",
      "The numerical-method context is high-resolution finite-volume methods based on solving Riemann problems (raw PDF page 30, printed page 8, Section 1.4, opening sentence).",
      "The adjoining interface discussion allows discontinuities in both medium and initial data and describes reflected and transmitted waves through the interface Riemann solution; it supplies motivation rather than an averaging formula (raw PDF page 30, printed page 8, Section 1.4: 'discontinuity in the medium' and 'effects of reflection and transmission')."
    ],
    "semantic_facets": [
      {
        "topic": "Cellwise assignment and variation",
        "details": [
          "Properties are assigned separately to each grid cell and may differ between cells (raw PDF page 30, printed page 8, Section 1.4: 'each grid cell can be assigned different material properties').",
          "The wording permits differing properties; it does not require pairwise unequal properties for every pair of cells (raw PDF page 30, printed page 8, Section 1.4: 'can be assigned different material properties')."
        ]
      },
      {
        "topic": "Material parameters versus solution averages",
        "details": [
          "The selected passage averages material parameters associated with the medium (raw PDF page 30, printed page 8, Section 1.4: 'material parameters').",
          "The explicit integral-divided-by-volume definition on the earlier page concerns the solution q; identifying this with the required averaging of material parameters would be an additional interpretation (raw PDF page 27, printed page 5, Section 1.2: 'cell average of q'; raw PDF page 30, printed page 8, Section 1.4: 'appropriate averaging')."
        ]
      },
      {
        "topic": "Strength of the source claim",
        "details": [
          "The passage asserts a modeling capability and qualitative suitability, rather than a numbered theorem or an equality involving averaged parameters (raw PDF page 30, printed page 8, Section 1.4: 'can be assigned' and 'a natural choice').",
          "No error estimate, convergence rate, uniqueness assertion, or preservation identity accompanies the selected averaging claim (raw PDF page 30, printed page 8, Section 1.4, sentence beginning 'Finite volume methods are a natural choice')."
        ]
      }
    ]
  },
  "undebatable_constraints": [
    "The assigned objects must represent material properties of individual grid cells, and the averaged inputs must represent material parameters of the medium (raw PDF page 30, printed page 8, Section 1.4: 'material properties' and 'material parameters').",
    "The spatial region for the stated averaging is the volume enclosed by the relevant cell (raw PDF page 30, printed page 8, Section 1.4: 'over the volume enclosed by the cell').",
    "The source leaves the appropriate material-averaging procedure unspecified; a particular arithmetic, harmonic, weighted, or other formula cannot be attributed to this sentence as an explicit assertion (raw PDF page 30, printed page 8, Section 1.4: 'an appropriate averaging').",
    "The explicit definition of the cell average of q must remain distinguished from the unspecified averaging of material parameters (raw PDF page 27, printed page 5, Section 1.2: 'cell average of q'; raw PDF page 30, printed page 8, Section 1.4: 'material parameters').",
    "The qualitative statement of suitability must retain its scope as a capability for heterogeneous media, without becoming a universal quantitative guarantee (raw PDF page 30, printed page 8, Section 1.4: 'can be an attractive alternative' and 'can be assigned')."
  ],
  "ambiguities": [
    {
      "issue": "The appropriate averaging operator is not specified.",
      "evidence": "Raw PDF page 30, printed page 8, Section 1.4 says 'an appropriate averaging of the material parameters'; raw PDF page 27, printed page 5, Section 1.2 explicitly supplies an integral-divided-by-volume formula only for q.",
      "impact": "The selected passage does not determine whether material averaging uses that formula, another mean, weights, or a procedure depending on the parameter and physical model."
    },
    {
      "issue": "The parameter space and relationship between parameters and assigned properties are unspecified.",
      "evidence": "Raw PDF page 30, printed page 8, Section 1.4 refers generically to 'material properties' and 'material parameters' without defining their types or a conversion between them.",
      "impact": "Scalar, vector, or tensor representations, componentwise treatment, and any conversion from averaged parameters to properties require additional modeling choices."
    },
    {
      "issue": "Conditions for a mathematically defined average are not stated.",
      "evidence": "Raw PDF page 30, printed page 8, Section 1.4 mentions 'the volume enclosed by the cell' but supplies no integrability, measurability, finiteness, or nonzero-volume conditions in the averaging sentence.",
      "impact": "A precise integral construction may need such assumptions, but the passage does not specify their form or exceptional-case behavior."
    },
    {
      "issue": "The criterion making an average appropriate is not defined.",
      "evidence": "Raw PDF page 30, printed page 8, Section 1.4 uses 'appropriate averaging' and then discusses interface Riemann solutions, reflection, and transmission without stating a condition connecting these to a particular average.",
      "impact": "The source does not establish that any chosen average preserves specified interface coefficients, effective wave speeds, conservation properties, or numerical accuracy."
    }
  ],
  "contract_plain_english": "In its discussion of wave propagation in heterogeneous media, the source says finite-volume methods are a natural choice because each grid cell can be assigned its own material properties through appropriate averaging of material parameters over the volume enclosed by that cell (raw PDF page 30, printed page 8, Section 1.4, first paragraph). Heterogeneity may vary slowly or include sharp interfaces with jumps in material parameters (raw PDF page 29, printed page 7, Section 1.4, final paragraph). This is a qualitative modeling claim. The material-averaging formula and its admissibility conditions are not specified by 'appropriate averaging' (raw PDF page 30, printed page 8). The earlier integral-divided-by-volume definition explicitly concerns the cell average of the solution q and does not by itself settle the material-averaging choice (raw PDF page 27, printed page 5, Section 1.2)."
}
